#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221203 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "03fbfe2c-b12f-5220-9fae-e0dfe210a505" :question/math-academy-id "e-21857" :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-2y=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim 3 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a particular solution has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) + b_n \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y'_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -{a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{0}=3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation,\n\n$$\na_0=-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means the constant term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation,\n$b_n=-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}.$\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\\right)\\dfrac{n\\pi}{3}-2a_n=\\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{216}{n^2\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $b_n$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼-\\frac{3}{2} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221204 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "3944f6e4-a3a8-5bad-87c5-18227ce223bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21858" :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation $y''-y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim 1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: We may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Because the forcing function has only a constant term and sine terms, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -b_n n^2\\pi^2 \\sin(n\\pi x)\n-\n\\left( \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(-n^2\\pi^2-1)\\sin(n\\pi x)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n![](images/0e/0e33aea74f67de3091fda6d168872458efb0acdba8a4d7b472fea9da1670df27.svg)\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \n-1\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\:\n\\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^3\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+1)}\n\\cdot\n\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221206 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/id #uuid "926606c2-1e1a-5aab-8f2e-826163a204a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21875" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y'' + 9\\pi^2 y = f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nWhat harmonic of the forcing function does the natural frequency of the system match?\nAnd what is the resonant component of a particular solution?" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+9\\pi^2 y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of\n\n$$\n\\omega=\\sqrt{9\\pi^2}=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $\\left(L=1\\right)$ are\n\n$$\n\\omega_n=\\dfrac{n\\pi}{1}=n\\pi.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have $\\omega_3=3\\pi.$ Since $\\omega_3=\\omega,$ the natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function. This is the answer to the first question.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\nf_3(x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=x(A\\cos(3\\pi x)+B\\sin(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_{p_3}$ and $y_{p_3}$ into the equation $y''+9\\pi^2 y=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\left[-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)-9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}\\right]+9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n![](images/24/245bf05fb907f9b62a62cda35b50ff309770378a611f4596e023355aec9df276.svg)\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=-\\dfrac{x}{54\\pi}\\cos(3\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eac6c01-7323-5cfa-8a28-9ee89e883eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(4+n\\pi )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315206} {:answer/id #uuid "182aace0-6eea-5683-b022-6752de8edb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}(16-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315259} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf9ea5e-bf43-5329-942f-18c9414f5dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{16+{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315643} {:answer/id #uuid "979d240d-8a78-5e5e-98eb-afae4adb2c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "979d240d-8a78-5e5e-98eb-afae4adb2c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315963} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aa9decd-5701-5de4-9020-d2ec143f0d30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2c6150b-3687-5b67-8114-c6babfa1ec19" :question/math-academy-id "q-343373" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+16y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+16({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(16-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\frac{144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{n}(144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{9}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d164cd9-05f8-521d-a1c1-4640e45ea11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430675} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d3c588-3230-5c65-b95d-a0c96715b76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430676} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bb3694-2f5f-53a7-8495-088462a1c3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430677} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8c507d-8e09-52ff-853e-45a9bff03a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2d3c588-3230-5c65-b95d-a0c96715b76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430676} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19ae7dd-5f1e-5b74-9ed5-f098fbd5c4d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19896dd2-dac8-5bfb-aec1-2f10cff206ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430672} {:answer/id #uuid "426d62eb-dc4e-5375-bd07-e202606e3cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430673} {:answer/id #uuid "61b492ca-50cf-52f7-8792-35c237dc1fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{8{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430674} {:answer/id #uuid "d8196464-3a2a-5b97-9d21-a30214f0b784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19896dd2-dac8-5bfb-aec1-2f10cff206ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430672} :answer-field/id #uuid "223bd29e-2a90-5e8f-a7b8-ad5a6d0bbcc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ad9a596-b8c8-5727-be8e-f1a040ae71ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-343382" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{4{\\pi }^{2}}=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=2\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x))+x(-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)-4{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)-4{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+4{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\pi A=0 \\\\ 4\\pi B=1\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c374525-0d10-5050-a300-a79dcfbe1344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315379} {:answer/id #uuid "30db2edc-6f08-58d8-870f-a953379c323f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315396} {:answer/id #uuid "68b802ec-a75e-510e-92f8-96b459e2052d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315709} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcf3171-8f8f-58d3-b834-e5adb23c8e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdcf3171-8f8f-58d3-b834-e5adb23c8e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316539} :answer-field/id #uuid "18004c40-7610-5c60-8808-ea9e4b8d8d4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f91b7c-20bf-51fd-a393-6190c6c8e355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315338} {:answer/id #uuid "3890626f-6d86-591f-9835-41dc0785b07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315443} {:answer/id #uuid "c23d6f0d-4068-5f82-916d-ff3b5f243150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3890626f-6d86-591f-9835-41dc0785b07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315443} :answer-field/id #uuid "58145300-29fc-5a90-a84c-4bf31179dc08" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316750} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0962404-85c9-5e1a-8305-8c121059d764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316458} {:answer/id #uuid "f48b2e93-6f81-57a4-9d61-664c45eb47e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316483} {:answer/id #uuid "f95e153a-270c-5fbb-9ed6-cc3b5cab48dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{24}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f48b2e93-6f81-57a4-9d61-664c45eb47e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316483} :answer-field/id #uuid "c413fdfb-1b7f-5850-8061-58d3e5250a37" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae77a2f6-2db6-5433-96eb-14517b11c7c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343854" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-2y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, ${b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5beaf63-a24e-5096-a60c-b0469d5c3b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397678} {:answer/id #uuid "abc5583a-c2d4-5270-9535-e8dc76611c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397679} {:answer/id #uuid "20b43c86-9df5-5ea5-b8db-701f1d2fcb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397680} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b221ad-c466-515a-9758-208b0b79820b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abc5583a-c2d4-5270-9535-e8dc76611c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397679} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa212b9-4a9e-5ed1-977d-8a0f92f187bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397677} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c55a33cd-7217-5f5d-aae1-44a553c062e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397683} {:answer/id #uuid "68fd5497-22fd-5e95-baa6-a3fb2a651897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{30(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397684} {:answer/id #uuid "9261cac5-a1b2-5b9e-85af-f8f060b21c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c55a33cd-7217-5f5d-aae1-44a553c062e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397683} :answer-field/id #uuid "685da459-566a-5cb9-907e-c163e95f44e3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397682} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52840280-13ca-54da-b4d4-afd74b42c883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397687} {:answer/id #uuid "7120f89a-088f-5258-8c2d-358cc96f6e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397688} {:answer/id #uuid "15b8dddf-399a-5ce8-a3a7-717ad28083fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7120f89a-088f-5258-8c2d-358cc96f6e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397688} :answer-field/id #uuid "615c3feb-87ab-595d-bd3c-9d3dd60c0bff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2c46dfd-7116-546d-b775-da0e3f310626" :question/math-academy-id "q-343860" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-5 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5(\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi })=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225} \\\\  & =\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dce7229-1a2d-5e8a-9b24-81a137c060a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (25-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368333} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a00450-0f8a-5ca2-a742-5dd6a893e2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (25-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368334} {:answer/id #uuid "083111de-394f-56a3-846e-6ab3897e79da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi (25-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368335} {:answer/id #uuid "ee32befe-9c82-59a6-bc8c-ed09d659e96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (25-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee32befe-9c82-59a6-bc8c-ed09d659e96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (25-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368336} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd85012-cb13-5af2-aa01-15ea4bd3072a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdf0a8e9-3b28-5328-8c13-b8779f952184" :question/math-academy-id "q-343876" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+25y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+25({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(25-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients:\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}(25-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (25-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (25-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5229753e-9f7f-5a27-b3ee-c4a1ce8dde6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315586} {:answer/id #uuid "862b0cf3-99de-5116-b9ab-82a11436ad9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315868} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dff446-91ec-5570-8171-c24135981f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316299} {:answer/id #uuid "d9600449-c9b9-5aec-ba8b-15aa0966e5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "862b0cf3-99de-5116-b9ab-82a11436ad9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315868} :answer-field/id #uuid "d87d8604-1b8f-56e7-a069-08f0654c46b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d996731-b251-5b34-875b-e57bb6c2b7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315290} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5d9269-3d3c-576a-b059-ab2e729a7735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315453} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cc4c52-e7e1-5f1b-ab95-62c5cbd519d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316484} {:answer/id #uuid "fce744b4-f140-59b2-84b8-c7432217b040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a5d9269-3d3c-576a-b059-ab2e729a7735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315453} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb46e197-216d-53cb-9e25-46577a9846a0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de35c174-f52d-54b1-9320-5816b5069f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343905" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{15}{16\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}]}
